      "content": "That makes sense. To get a bit more specific for my literature review, i'm seeing a lot of discussion in certain niche academic circles about the \"evolutionary mismatch\" hypothesis applied to different ethnic groups. \n\nSpecifically, some theorists argue that certain populations adapted to high-competition or high-conflict environments, leading to a phenotypic expression of higher impulsivity or aggression as a survival mechanism, whereas other lineages may have evolved in more stable, cooperative-heavy environments that selected for higher social cohesion and lower risk-taking. \n\nCould you elaborate on what the actual data or theoretical models say about these specific trade-offs? I'm trying to differentiate between the arguments that frame these as \"maladaptive\" in modern society versus those that see them as simply distinct, biologically rooted strategic variations between different human populations.",
